      ":question/problem": "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 5 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 0 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that by replacing $a_{31}$, $a_{42}$, and $a_{43}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 5 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 0 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\longrightarrow\\,\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 5 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe nonzero rows have leading entries in columns 1, 2, 3, 4, in strictly increasing order, and all zero rows are at the bottom. The other options will not give us a matrix in row echelon form.",
